/-!
# Dimension formula over a universally catenary domain

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 02IJ, equality clause
(blueprint: lem-exc-dimension-formula-universally-catenary-finite-type-domain).

Residue fields use Mathlib's `IsFractionRing (A ⧸ p) p.ResidueField` instance.
Transcendence degrees are mapped to extended natural numbers by `Cardinal.toENat`;
they are finite here, so this retains their ordinary numerical values.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace IsUniversallyCatenary

/-- The dimension formula for a finite type inclusion of Noetherian universally catenary
commutative domains, at a prime and its contraction. Source: Stacks Project, Algebra,
Tag 02IJ, equality clause
(blueprint: lem-exc-dimension-formula-universally-catenary-finite-type-domain).

Proof (the nine steps of the DAG, following Tag 02IJ):
1. For a field F, lem-pur-polynomial-field-pid shows F[T] is a PID. A nonzero prime
   is generated by an irreducible polynomial, so it is maximal and has height one;
   the zero prime has height zero. At zero the residue field is F(T), with basis {T}
   in the sense of def-weil-pbc-transcendence-basis. At a nonzero prime the residue
   field is F(theta), algebraic by lem-weil-pbc-simple-algebraic-extension-power-basis,
   and has degree zero by lem-kun-transcendence-degree-tower. Thus height plus residue
   transcendence degree is one in both cases (def-kun-transcendence-degree).
2. Set P = R[T], F = Frac(R), and G = Frac(P). The polynomial-domain lemma
   lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-domain permits fractions. Clearing coefficient
   denominators gives G = F(T). Fractions are algebraic over their original domains
   by bY-a. Apply lem-exc-domain-transcendence-degree-tower to R/P/G and R/F/G:
   def-kun-transcendence-degree and step 1 give trdeg_R P = 1.
3. By def-weil-polynomial-ring, P is free over R, hence flat by
   lem-weil-pbc-projective-module-flat. It is finite type by
   def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra and Noetherian by
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian. Apply lem-weil-pbc-flat-height-formula
   at q over p. Reduction of coefficients identifies P/pP with (R/p)[T].
4. Localize (R/p)[T] at the nonzero coefficients to obtain kappa(p)[T]. The common
   denominator construction verifies localization. By
   lem-weil-pbc-localization-prime-residue-field-iso, chains below q/pP correspond
   to chains below its extension Q, since all these primes avoid the denominators.
   Heights agree, and its residue-field clause identifies kappa(q) with kappa(Q)
   over kappa(p). Steps 1-3 give the desired polynomial formula.
5. For a one-generator domain S = R[theta] with nonzero prime kernel n in P, the
   inclusion implies n contracts to zero. A nonzero element of n annihilates theta
   over F, so lem-weil-pbc-simple-algebraic-extension-power-basis makes F(theta)
   finite over F. Clearing coefficients makes S algebraic over R; hence its degree
   is zero by def-kun-transcendence-degree. The inverse image q' of q has the same
   quotient and residue field as q.
6. Localize P to F[T]. By lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-domain a nonzero element of n
   stays nonzero. The prime correspondence in
   lem-weil-pbc-localization-prime-residue-field-iso identifies all chains below n
   with those below its nonzero extension. Step 1 gives height(n) = 1.
7. By def-exc-excellent-ring and def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra, P is catenary.
   Bounded finite chain lengths permit saturated refinements. Prefix a saturated
   chain from n to q' with the saturated inclusion 0 < n. Catenarity makes its
   length height(q'). Quotient prime correspondence identifies chains from n to q'
   with chains from 0 to q. Thus height(q') = height(q) + 1, including q' = n.
8. Substitute this identity and step 5 into step 4. Heights are finite by the chain
   bounds in def-exc-excellent-ring, and the residue degree is zero or one by step 1.
   Cancel one to obtain the formula for a one-generator quotient, hence for every
   one-generator domain extension.
9. Induct on algebra generators (def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra). With none, S = R
   and all degrees vanish. Otherwise take the intermediate algebra generated by all
   but the last generator. It is a Noetherian domain by
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian, and universally catenary by composing
   finite generator lists in def-exc-excellent-ring. Apply induction and step 8.
   Add the two formulas and cancel the finite intermediate height. The identities
   lem-kun-transcendence-degree-tower and lem-exc-domain-transcendence-degree-tower
   identify the two sums of degrees, yielding the asserted formula.
-/
theorem height_add_trdeg_residueField_eq
    {R : Type u} {S : Type v} [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [IsDomain R] [IsDomain S]
    [Algebra R S] [IsNoetherianRing R] [Algebra.FiniteType R S]
    (hR : IsUniversallyCatenary R) (hinj : Function.Injective (algebraMap R S))
    (p : Ideal R) (q : Ideal S) [p.IsPrime] [q.IsPrime]
    (hpq : p = q.comap (algebraMap R S)) :
    q.height + Cardinal.toENat
        (@Algebra.trdeg p.ResidueField q.ResidueField _ _
          (Ideal.ResidueField.map p q (algebraMap R S) hpq).toAlgebra) =
      p.height + Cardinal.toENat (Algebra.trdeg R S) := by
  sorry

end IsUniversallyCatenary
